<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    The operator console's iOS code-signing entitlements. Referenced by the shared
    XcodeGen template (scripts/ios/project.yml, `entitlements > path`) and installed
    into the generated project by ios-postinit's Patch H; `just ios-template-check`
    guards the wiring.

    Deliberately ABSENT: any cloud-container entitlement. That capability belongs only
    to the wallet's media backup; the console's Secure-Enclave key and keychain items
    need none, and the template-check gate enforces this stays true.

    Keychain access groups — same design as the wallet's (ADR-0030), console-scoped.

    Without this key every console Keychain item lands in the IMPLICIT group
    `$(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.malpercio.admincompanion`, i.e. the access group IS the
    bundle id — and, more immediately, the Notification Service Extension (a separate
    bundle) could never read the notification key and pinned sender keys the app writes.

    ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. iOS files a new item into the FIRST entry when the caller
    does not name a group, and searches EVERY entitled group on an unqualified read. So:

      [0] org.obsign.admin.shared — the stable group, decoupled from the bundle id.
                                    New items are written here; the NSE is entitled to
                                    exactly this group and nothing else.
      [1] dev.malpercio.…         — the legacy (implicit-until-now) group. Every item a
                                    pre-notification install wrote — the device admin
                                    key, the pairing document, the biometric pref —
                                    lives here, so it must stay listed indefinitely or
                                    those installs come up looking factory-reset.

    Deliberately its OWN stable group, not the wallet's `org.obsign.shared`: the two
    apps' items never need to see each other, and one shared group would let either
    app's extension read the other app's notification private key.

    No Apple Developer portal work for this key — a provisioning profile authorizes the
    whole team prefix via a <TeamID>.* wildcard.
  -->
  <key>keychain-access-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)org.obsign.admin.shared</string>
    <string>$(AppIdentifierPrefix)dev.malpercio.admincompanion</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    Push notifications — the operator alert channel (flagged accounts, storage warnings).
    Without this key `registerForRemoteNotifications` never yields a device token and the
    console half of the notification relay is inert. `development` is correct for debug
    builds and TestFlight; Xcode's App Store export rewrites it to `production`.

    PORTAL WORK, same as the wallet's: enable the Push Notifications capability on this
    App ID and regenerate the provisioning profile, or signing fails with an entitlements
    mismatch. The official relay must also serve this bundle id as an APNs topic.
  -->
  <key>aps-environment</key>
  <string>development</string>
</dict>
</plist>
